blocks:
  - type: metin
    metin: |-
      Famagusta is a place where two languages run through daily life together. University campuses, foreign nationals who have lived on the island for years, and people who grew up bilingual all share the same city. One of the first questions that comes up when looking for therapy is exactly this: in which language?

      The question looks practical, but it is not. In therapy language is not merely a medium of transfer; it determines directly how easily you can say what.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Why does language matter so much in therapy?
    metin: |-
      Emotional vocabulary is richer in a first language for most people. The difference between "I am sad" and "there is a tightness in my chest" can only be drawn once you have command of a language's finer settings. A session running in a first language therefore often deepens more quickly.

      On the other hand, the distance a second language provides is sometimes useful. Some people find recounting a difficult memory in their first language too close, and speaking in a second language opens a gap they can breathe in. Both are valid; which suits you may not be clear until you try.
  - type: kart-yigini
    baslik: Which one, when?
    giris: 'There is no fixed rule, but these tendencies are common:'
    kartlar:
      - baslik: Working in your first language
        metin: On matters of emotion, childhood, family and identity, a first language usually moves faster. When crying, when angry, or when recalling a memory, the mind often returns to it on its own.
      - baslik: Working in a second language
        metin: If you conduct your daily life in that language — university, work, social circle — defining problems in it can feel more consistent. The distance it provides can also make it easier to begin on a subject that is hard to speak about.
      - baslik: Using both together
        metin: Switching language mid-sentence, leaving a word untranslated, or saying an idiom as it stands are not problems in therapy. For bilingual individuals this is often the most natural flow.
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: yesil
    baslik: English sessions run directly, not through an interpreter
    metin: At our centre English sessions are held directly with the psychologist, with no interpreter in between. Because the tone of what is said, the pauses and the particular word chosen carry at least as much information as the content, this difference is decisive. For international students newly arrived on the island and for foreign nationals, it removes the language distance altogether.
  - type: metin
    baslik: Online psychotherapy in Turkish and in English
    metin: |-
      The language of a session and its format are independent of each other. Both Turkish and English online psychotherapy can be conducted by video, and in terms of language there is no difference from an in-person session.

      The online format is particularly useful for two groups. First, people living outside Famagusta who struggle to reach a psychologist working in the language they need. Second, those who have left the country but want to continue the process in their own language — students who graduate and move on, or individuals relocating abroad. How video sessions run is set out in [online therapy in North Cyprus](/en/blog/online-therapy-north-cyprus.html).
  - type: metin
    baslik: Why is English psychotherapy limited in Famagusta?
    metin: |-
      Some psychologists working in North Cyprus hold sessions in one language only. That narrows the available pool noticeably for anyone searching in English — especially when experience in a particular area is also required.

      For someone looking for English psychotherapy in Famagusta, the practical route is this: ask about language first, then about the subject to be worked on. A psychologist offering sessions in English and a psychologist who has carried out clinical work in that language are not the same thing. What else to look for is covered in [finding a psychologist in North Cyprus](/en/blog/find-psychologist-north-cyprus.html).
  - type: bilgi-kutusu
    ton: turuncu
    baslik: Changing language mid-process
    metin: You are not obliged to continue in the language you began in. Some people find it easier to discuss cognitive and practical matters in one language and things touching childhood and family in another. The shift itself usually carries information — which subject feels as though it belongs to which language is a detail worth pausing on.
  - type: metin
    baslik: 'In short: the right language is the one you let go in'
    metin: |-
      There is no test for choosing a language. The most useful measure is this: in which language do you stop thinking about how to build the sentence? Therapy deepens when you are speaking without deliberating, not while searching for words.

      If you are unsure, that is what the first session is for. Sessions at our centre run in Turkish or English, and [online therapy](/en/services/online-therapy.html) is available in both. We can decide together which to start with.
